BT /F1 16 Tf 72 720 Td (Chapter One. The Ledger) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 690 Td (A ledger is a book of accounts. Each account has a page, and each page has two) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 676 Td (sides: what came in on the left, what went out on the right. The habit is older than) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 662 Td (printing, and it has outlived every machine that was supposed to replace it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 648 Td (The first rule of the ledger is that nothing is written in it directly. Every) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 634 Td (figure arrives from somewhere else, from a journal or a day book, and carries with it) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 620 Td (the date and the reference of the entry it came from.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 606 Td (The second rule is that every figure is written twice. A sale is a credit to) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 592 Td (sales and a debit to the customer; a payment is a credit to cash and a debit to the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 578 Td (supplier. The two sides must agree, or the book is wrong.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 564 Td (Because of the second rule a clerk can test the whole book with one addition. If) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 550 Td (the debits and the credits of every page are added up, the two totals must be equal.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 536 Td (When they are not, an error has been made somewhere in the posting.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 522 Td (Equality proves less than it seems to. A figure posted to the wrong account, or a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 508 Td (transaction left out altogether, leaves the totals in balance. The test catches a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 494 Td (slip of the pen; it does not catch a mistake of judgement.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 480 Td (The ledger therefore needs a reader as well as a writer. Someone must look along) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 466 Td (each page and ask whether the entries make sense: whether rent is paid each month,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 452 Td (whether a customer who owes money is still buying, whether a balance has grown that) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 438 Td (should have shrunk.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 424 Td (This reading is slow work, and it is the part of bookkeeping that the old manuals) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 410 Td (praise most. A merchant who reads his own ledger, they say, knows his business better) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 396 Td (than one who only signs the totals at the end of the year.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 382 Td (The page of an account is arranged for that reading. Dates run down the left) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 368 Td (margin, a short description follows, then the reference, then the amount. A glance) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 354 Td (down the amounts shows the rhythm of the account; a glance down the descriptions) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 340 Td (shows its story.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 326 Td (Balances are carried forward from page to page. When a page is full the clerk) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 312 Td (totals both sides, writes the difference on the smaller side as the balance carried) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 298 Td (down, and begins the next page with the same figure brought down on the other side.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 284 Td (At the year end every account is closed in the same way. Revenue and expense) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 270 Td (accounts are emptied into a single account of profit and loss, and what remains in) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 256 Td (that account is the result of the year, ready to be carried to the owner.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 242 Td (The balances that survive the closing are the assets, the debts and the owner\222s) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 228 Td (interest. Listed together they form the balance sheet, which is nothing more than the) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 214 Td (ledger seen from a distance, with the detail folded away.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 200 Td (It follows that a balance sheet can never be better than the ledger beneath it. A) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 186 Td (tidy statement drawn from a careless book is still a careless book, and an auditor) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 172 Td (who wants to know the truth of the statement goes back to the pages.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 158 Td (That is the reason the ledger is kept in ink and never erased. A wrong figure is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 144 Td (corrected by a new entry that reverses it, so that the page shows both the mistake) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 130 Td (and its correction, and the reader can follow what happened and when.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 90 116 Td (A ledger kept this way is a record of decisions as much as of money. Each line) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 102 Td (says that somebody, on a certain day, judged a transaction to belong to a certain) Tj ET
